  "id": "ops_and_numbers",
  "description": "canonicalize_document rewrites the deprecated ops (esm-spec §4.2): neg with one operand is unary -, pow with two is ^, a broadcast of neg with one operand maps -, and a use with the wrong operand count, or inside a free-form object, is left as written. A faq drops its default reduce \"+\" and semiring \"sum_product\" (esm-spec §4.8.2), and keeps both when either is anything else. Every number, free-form objects included, takes its canonical form (CONFORMANCE §5.5.3.1): a decimal token is rounded to binary64, an integral double in Int64 is an integer (2.0 is 2, 1.0000000000000001 is 1, -1.999999999999999e16 is -19999999999999992, -0.0 is 0), an integer token in Int64 is kept exactly (-19999999999999990), and any other double is a float with its shortest round-trip digits (1.4715177646857694 is 1.4715177646857693, 4.940656458412465e-324 is 5.0e-324, 1.0e19 stays a float). Compare numbers by type and value: 2 and 2.0 differ. A binding whose JSON reader holds every number as a double cannot hold -19999999999999990 and reads it as -19999999999999992 on both sides. Generated by scripts/gen-canonical-unit-spelling.py; expected is what esm-lean canon writes.",
